  does distance predict discrimination? Spearman rank correlation
  over all 28 pairs, between the gap and the voice score:
    Hz  distance between the two WORDS      rho +0.075
    mel distance between the two WORDS      rho +0.051
    mel distance between the two ECHOES     rho +0.563  <- what is scored
  THE LIMIT IS THE VOICE, NOT THE EAR — how far apart the creature
  SAYS two words predicts discrimination better than how far apart
  they are. A pair is hard when this larynx cannot say them
  differently, so a larger vocabulary needs a wider reachable vowel
  space and not a finer cochlea.
  NEITHER SEPARATES — the two metrics rank these eight vowels too
  similarly to tell apart (+0.075 against +0.051). Deciding this needs
  vowels chosen so the two orderings DISAGREE, which these were not.

  3 creatures, 28 pairs, scored on the ARTICULATORS: mean 0.512,
  0 of 28 at or above 0.75. Unguarded it would read 0.720.

  126-dimension shuffled control: 0.127 (must sit at 0.125 — a
    nearest-centroid estimator can gain from width alone)

  one of eight on VOCAL'S OWN SPIKES:  0.234 (chance 0.125)
    against 0.253 through the nine motor groups. Same estimator,
    same trials, same split; the only change is which vector a
    trial is. The module does NOT hold what the knobs miss, so the
    ceiling is upstream of the larynx, not in its
    parameterisation.

  one of eight on the ARTICULATORS: 0.125 (chance 0.125)
    <- THE CLAIM. Drops loudness and voicing, which the row below
    does not, and M1d made the answering burst louder by
    construction — so an unguarded vocabulary score is exactly
    where a spurious gain would land.
  one of eight, nearest centroid on held-out trials: 0.253 (chance 0.125)

  A pairwise table saying every pair is separable does NOT say a word
  can be picked out of eight — that needs every boundary to hold at once.
  The gap between the two numbers above is the interesting part.

  THE VOCABULARY IS FULL BELOW EIGHT — 28 of 28 pairs are under 0.75
  (worst 0.764 against a chance of 0.5), and one of eight reads 0.253
  against a chance of 0.125. Four words hold apart on every pair; eight
  do not, and the hardest rows above say where it runs out.
  [vocab took 94.9 s]

